1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,956
Sam, would you come in, please? Sam

2
00:00:05,956 --> 00:00:09,601
You sound like something's bothering you, Susan.

3
00:00:09,601 --> 00:00:13,912
The sketches for the cover of the new doll book?

4
00:00:13,912 --> 00:00:14,287
That's not it.

5
00:00:14,287 --> 00:00:14,662
Please sit down.

6
00:00:14,662 --> 00:00:14,862
Sure.

7
00:00:14,787 --> 00:00:22,196
I need your advice on a personsal matter,

8
00:00:22,196 --> 00:00:24,224
but it's not about me.

9
00:00:24,224 --> 00:00:28,029
You need my advice on a personal matter,

10
00:00:28,029 --> 00:00:31,780
and it's not about you.

11
00:00:31,780 --> 00:00:33,718
It's about my grandfather.

12
00:00:33,718 --> 00:00:35,736
What's the problem?

13
00:00:35,736 --> 00:00:38,714
It won't sound like a big deal,

14
00:00:38,714 --> 00:00:39,434
but it is.

15
00:00:39,434 --> 00:00:40,874
I had dinner with him Saturday,

16
00:00:40,874 --> 00:00:44,372
and he's very unhappy about not working.

17
00:00:44,372 --> 00:00:45,197
I thought he was retired

18
00:00:45,197 --> 00:00:47,647
and pleased to be living with the family.

19
00:00:47,647 --> 00:00:48,314
He is,

20
00:00:48,314 --> 00:00:51,458
but there's so much energy and talent in the man,

21
00:00:51,458 --> 00:00:53,125
and he doesn't get to use it.

22
00:00:53,125 --> 00:00:54,603
But what can I do?

23
00:00:54,603 --> 00:00:56,581
What kind of advice are you looking for?

24
00:00:56,581 --> 00:00:59,061
Simply this.

25
00:00:59,061 --> 00:01:01,388
John Marchetta runs this company. John Marchetta

26
00:01:01,388 --> 00:01:03,195
He founded this company.

27
00:01:03,195 --> 00:01:03,524
Right.

28
00:01:03,524 --> 00:01:07,229
John Marchetta gave me my start here six years ago,

29
00:01:07,229 --> 00:01:09,633
When I first graduated from college.

30
00:01:09,633 --> 00:01:12,804
He gave me the chance to use my talents

31
00:01:12,804 --> 00:01:17,427
and made me feel more confident.

32
00:01:17,427 --> 00:01:18,427
Right.

33
00:01:18,427 --> 00:01:20,405
Maybe he can do the same thing for your grandfather.

34
00:01:20,405 --> 00:01:23,383
Or at least give him some advice.

35
00:01:23,383 --> 00:01:27,133
Right.

36
00:01:27,133 --> 00:01:29,339
Then I've solved your problem.

37
00:01:29,339 --> 00:01:32,317
I can always depend on you, Sam.

38
00:01:32,317 --> 00:01:34,317
I'm glad to help.

39
00:01:34,317 --> 00:01:36,495
Shall I call Mr. Marchetta for you?

40
00:01:36,495 --> 00:01:38,273
No, no. I'll do that.

41
00:01:38,273 --> 00:01:44,813
Thanks.

42
00:01:44,813 --> 00:01:48,207
Now, how's the Stewart family?

43
00:01:48,207 --> 00:01:50,185
Fine, thank you, Mr. Mrarchetta.

44
00:01:50,185 --> 00:01:52,585
Except for my grandfather.

45
00:01:52,585 --> 00:01:54,496
What's wrong, Susan?

46
00:01:54,496 --> 00:01:55,663
What's wrong with him?

47
00:01:55,663 --> 00:01:57,500
He needs to work.

48
00:01:57,500 --> 00:02:04,278
In fact, that is the reason why I'm here to see you.

49
00:02:04,278 --> 00:02:08,629
I know you're building a new factory,

50
00:02:08,629 --> 00:02:10,938
and I thought ... maybe ...

51
00:02:10,938 --> 00:02:14,008
my grandfather is

52
00:02:14,008 --> 00:02:16,258
so experienced in the construction trade,

53
00:02:16,258 --> 00:02:18,166
he could be so valuable.

54
00:02:18,166 --> 00:02:21,678
Tell him to come and see me

55
00:02:21,678 --> 00:02:23,942
at ten o'clock tomorrow morning.

56
00:02:23,942 --> 00:02:27,795
I have an idea that may solve the problem for him

57
00:02:27,795 --> 00:02:29,898
and help a lot of other people.

58
00:02:29,898 --> 00:02:31,098
Really, Mr. Marchetta?

59
00:02:31,098 --> 00:02:32,607
Can I tell him that?

60
00:02:32,607 --> 00:02:33,094
Sure can.

61
00:02:33,094 --> 00:02:34,854
Ten o'clock in the morning. Here.

62
00:02:34,854 --> 00:02:37,854
Oh, thank you!
